91.text:
 POST /roxy/v1/enrol with Authorization: Bearer <enrolment token>. {
  "roxy_version": "0.1.0",
  "protocol_version": 1
} fieldmeaningroxy_versionthe node's roxy release; a server may refuse one below its floor with 426protocol_version1; a server that does not speak it answers 426 with missing: ["protocol_version:1"] {
  "node_id": "node-7f3a9c",
  "token": "nt1-9f86d081884c7d659a2feaa0c55ad015a3bf4f1b2b0b822cd15d6c15b0f00a08",
  "not_after": "2026-11-05T10:12:00Z",
  "renew_after_seconds": 1296000
} fieldmeaningnode_idchosen by the server: 1 to 128 characters from A-Z a-z 0-9 . _ -; stable for the life of the nodetokenthe node token: 1 to 512 bytes of RFC 6750 b64token text (A-Z a-z 0-9 - . _ ~ + /, then any number of =), opaque to the node, which stores it and presents it unchanged as Authorization: Bearer on every later request. Its lifetime and storage (hashed at rest is recommended) are the server's decisionsnot_afterwhen the server stops accepting the token, RFC 3339 in UTCrenew_after_secondscounted from receipt; once passed, the node renews. Well inside the token lifetime, so a failed renewal can be retried. Whatever the value, the node renews no later than two thirds of the way to not_after and no sooner than one second after receipt; it does not check the schema's minimum, so 0 renews once a second The enrolment token's lifetime and reuse (single-use, shared across a
fleet) are the control plane's decisions; single-use with a short expiry
is recommended. The node writes the node token, with its node_id and
not_after, to its state directory (mode 0600): the only credential the
protocol puts on disk, and a revocable per-node identity.

93.text:
 POST /roxy/v1/lease with the node token. The body is the node's state: {
  "lease_id": "lease-01J9Z8K3",
  "roxy_version": "0.1.0",
  "protocol_version": 1,
  "uptime_seconds": 86400,
  "policy_state": "loaded",
  "spooled_bytes": 0,
  "seq_unreserved": false,
  "quarantine": null
} fieldmeaninglease_idthe lease the node holds; null before the first. Lets the server notice a node that did not apply what it was sentroxy_versionthe node's release; the server may refuse to serve it with 426policy_statenone (no lease yet), loaded (a lease is in force) or expired (the lease ran down, or the node is revoked). A quarantine does not change it: the lease is still applied, and quarantine says the node refuses everything regardlessspooled_bytesflow-log data accepted but not yet acknowledgedseq_unreservedtrue while events are being tagged with seq values past the node's persisted reservation, because a flow.seq write is failing or has not caught up. A restart in that state may reuse those seq values, and the server would drop the reused events as duplicates; a node reporting true for long needs its state directory looked at. Absent from a node that predates the fieldquarantinethe quarantine in force, or null. Absent from a node that predates the field {
  "id": "q-01J9ZB7QX4R2M8K3N5P6T7V8W9",
  "since": "2026-10-13T09:14:02Z",
  "cause": {"kind": "rule", "rule": "exfil-guard", "flow": "01J9ZB7P"}
} fieldmeaningidminted by the node per incident; what a release namessincewhen it tripped, on the node's clockcausewhat tripped it, as the node's quarantined flow event carries it: kind: rule with the rule id and the flow, or kind: threshold with the listener, the counter (parse_errors), its count and window_seconds (flow log) The server answers 200 with the full lease on every poll (no conditional
fetch), or 410, 401, 426, 5xx as in the
errors table.

